00:00:02.960 --> 00:00:04.510
So, in this video, I just want to cover

00:00:04.520 --> 00:00:07.750
very briefly how simulations work um how

00:00:07.760 --> 00:00:10.590
you how in digital systems.

00:00:10.600 --> 00:00:12.430
So, to do that, we've got here a

00:00:12.440 --> 00:00:15.470
waveform for a simple half adder with

00:00:15.480 --> 00:00:18.590
two inputs A and B and two outputs the

00:00:18.600 --> 00:00:20.670
sum and carry signal.

00:00:20.680 --> 00:00:23.150
So, we well, we the terminology we use,

00:00:23.160 --> 00:00:24.910
the attribute is first of all events.

00:00:24.920 --> 00:00:27.230
The an idea of an event is a change in

00:00:27.240 --> 00:00:29.350
some value in a circuit.

00:00:29.360 --> 00:00:31.430
So, for example, in the up here, we've

00:00:31.440 --> 00:00:33.790
got that the input A, the event is that

00:00:33.800 --> 00:00:37.710
it changes from a one to a zero. So,

00:00:37.720 --> 00:00:41.430
okay. Um and of course, inputs events on

00:00:41.440 --> 00:00:44.590
input signals may then produce events or

00:00:44.600 --> 00:00:47.590
other signals subsequently.

00:00:47.600 --> 00:00:49.790
But, there's a propagation delay. So,

00:00:49.800 --> 00:00:52.190
the output there's a period of time to

00:00:52.200 --> 00:00:54.430
in the input signals to propagate to

00:00:54.440 --> 00:00:56.590
changing the output. So, we have to take

00:00:56.600 --> 00:00:58.190
account of propagation delay when we're

00:00:58.200 --> 00:01:00.710
doing simulations. Real circuits have

00:01:00.720 --> 00:01:02.430
capacitance, they take some time to

00:01:02.440 --> 00:01:04.030
change

00:01:04.040 --> 00:01:06.590
um to switch states.

00:01:06.600 --> 00:01:08.830
So, changes in inputs to a gate do not

00:01:08.840 --> 00:01:10.510
produce immediate changes on the

00:01:10.520 --> 00:01:12.110
outputs. They take some time, and we

00:01:12.120 --> 00:01:14.390
have to simulate that.

00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:15.830
Um the other thing we have to be aware

00:01:15.840 --> 00:01:18.550
of is this concurrency of operation that

00:01:18.560 --> 00:01:21.830
an input event can change more than one

00:01:21.840 --> 00:01:23.270
can change more than one signal at a

00:01:23.280 --> 00:01:24.310
time.

00:01:24.320 --> 00:01:26.070
So, it can result in two gates changing

00:01:26.080 --> 00:01:27.870
concurrently or more.

00:01:27.880 --> 00:01:29.870
Um in this case, the two outputs sum and

00:01:29.880 --> 00:01:32.070
carry are both going to change going to

00:01:32.080 --> 00:01:34.270
change if A inputs from a one to zero in

00:01:34.280 --> 00:01:36.670
this particular circuit. So, how do we

00:01:36.680 --> 00:01:38.910
simulate that? Well, we're we're we

00:01:38.920 --> 00:01:41.310
simulate it by modeling

00:01:41.320 --> 00:01:44.390
the system as a in terms of the

00:01:44.400 --> 00:01:46.630
generation of events.

00:01:46.640 --> 00:01:49.310
Uh and it's what's called it's basically

00:01:49.320 --> 00:01:51.270
called discrete event simulation. That's

00:01:51.280 --> 00:01:51.910
the

00:01:51.920 --> 00:01:54.390
the the technique we use to accurately

00:01:54.400 --> 00:01:56.030
model the generation and ordering of the

00:01:56.040 --> 00:01:57.230
events.

00:01:57.240 --> 00:01:59.430
So, the the idea is we have a list of

00:01:59.440 --> 00:02:02.350
events in order in time.

00:02:02.360 --> 00:02:04.070
So, an ordered list of future events in

00:02:04.080 --> 00:02:05.630
the system.

00:02:05.640 --> 00:02:07.150
Each event describes a signal

00:02:07.160 --> 00:02:09.109
transition. So, here So, we talk about

00:02:09.119 --> 00:02:10.389
the head, that's going to be the next

00:02:10.399 --> 00:02:13.870
event. We've got a transition V1 to V2,

00:02:13.880 --> 00:02:15.590
and we've got a time when it occurs, in

00:02:15.600 --> 00:02:17.870
this case 5 ns. So, each event is

00:02:17.880 --> 00:02:20.430
described by a signal transition

00:02:20.440 --> 00:02:22.590
and the time it takes to happen. So, it

00:02:22.600 --> 00:02:26.150
takes 5 ns or 10 ns or whatever.

00:02:26.160 --> 00:02:27.990
And there's a value of a simulator clock

00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:30.230
which goes along which is referred to as

00:02:30.240 --> 00:02:32.270
the time step.

00:02:32.280 --> 00:02:34.830
And the value of all signals, of course,

00:02:34.840 --> 00:02:37.510
at the current time represent the this

00:02:37.520 --> 00:02:39.150
current state. So, you keep keep a

00:02:39.160 --> 00:02:40.510
record of the current state of the

00:02:40.520 --> 00:02:44.270
system, and you have this list of future

00:02:44.280 --> 00:02:45.830
events which will then change that

00:02:45.840 --> 00:02:47.750
state.

00:02:47.760 --> 00:02:49.270
So, that's the basic

00:02:49.280 --> 00:02:50.790
very simple

00:02:50.800 --> 00:02:54.110
approach. Um here we have an example. Um

00:02:54.120 --> 00:02:55.830
so, first thing is in this example, I'm

00:02:55.840 --> 00:02:57.830
going to assume that the gate

00:02:57.840 --> 00:03:00.949
propagation is 5 ns.

00:03:00.959 --> 00:03:02.082
Um

00:03:02.519 --> 00:03:04.910
Ma- Many Many things can happen at the

00:03:04.920 --> 00:03:08.190
same same time step. In reality, the the

00:03:08.200 --> 00:03:09.630
simulator will actually do them one

00:03:09.640 --> 00:03:11.790
after each other. Um it isn't doing them

00:03:11.800 --> 00:03:13.990
concurrently, it's running on on a um a

00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:16.229
single a processor.

00:03:16.239 --> 00:03:18.390
Um the order in which they occur, by the

00:03:18.400 --> 00:03:20.870
way, is not necessarily predetermined,

00:03:20.880 --> 00:03:22.670
either. Um it depends on how on the

00:03:22.680 --> 00:03:24.470
implementation. So, that that is one of

00:03:24.480 --> 00:03:26.972
the limitations of simulations.

00:03:26.982 --> 00:03:27.750
[sighs and gasps]

00:03:27.760 --> 00:03:28.910
Um

00:03:28.920 --> 00:03:30.990
So, basically, what we do is we start In

00:03:31.000 --> 00:03:33.229
this case, we start uh at time zero.

00:03:33.239 --> 00:03:34.750
We've got our two

00:03:34.760 --> 00:03:37.270
uh transitions which are actually that

00:03:37.280 --> 00:03:39.470
the outputs start in some undetermined

00:03:39.480 --> 00:03:42.750
state and will switch to a a determined

00:03:42.760 --> 00:03:45.509
state, right? It will switch state. So,

00:03:45.519 --> 00:03:47.150
that's the what we happen happen at

00:03:47.160 --> 00:03:48.830
zero. There's the five All right, we

00:03:48.840 --> 00:03:50.630
have the two events as when the sum and

00:03:50.640 --> 00:03:51.750
carry

00:03:51.760 --> 00:03:54.110
will change state.

00:03:54.120 --> 00:03:56.390
And I've just assumed that's 5 ns. It's

00:03:56.400 --> 00:03:58.190
not not nothing to do with the inputs,

00:03:58.200 --> 00:03:59.910
it's just when they change state. So,

00:03:59.920 --> 00:04:01.350
that will change that that will then

00:04:01.360 --> 00:04:03.710
result in the two outputs changing as

00:04:03.720 --> 00:04:04.990
shown here.

00:04:05.000 --> 00:04:06.310
Then what we have is we have an

00:04:06.320 --> 00:04:09.350
injection of the input events, right?

00:04:09.360 --> 00:04:11.590
So, basically, we have a number of input

00:04:11.600 --> 00:04:13.550
events. Here we have input events when A

00:04:13.560 --> 00:04:15.670
goes from 1 to 0.

00:04:15.680 --> 00:04:17.630
And then later on, we have two input

00:04:17.640 --> 00:04:20.150
events where A goes back to 1 and B goes

00:04:20.160 --> 00:04:23.470
down to 0. So, we inject an event where

00:04:23.480 --> 00:04:24.550
where

00:04:24.560 --> 00:04:27.350
which where the And it's after 5 ns. It

00:04:27.360 --> 00:04:29.590
happens to be after 5 ns.

00:04:29.600 --> 00:04:31.950
Uh where 1 goes to 0. So, what happens

00:04:31.960 --> 00:04:34.310
is when we we we then execute that that

00:04:34.320 --> 00:04:36.350
event when it goes to 1 or 0, we create

00:04:36.360 --> 00:04:38.550
two new events,

00:04:38.560 --> 00:04:41.590
right? 5 ns later to show that the two

00:04:41.600 --> 00:04:43.550
outputs will change. And we add those to

00:04:43.560 --> 00:04:44.950
the list,

00:04:44.960 --> 00:04:45.830
right?

00:04:45.840 --> 00:04:47.710
And at the same time, we've also got two

00:04:47.720 --> 00:04:49.430
in these two input events which will

00:04:49.440 --> 00:04:52.750
also be after 5 ns where A changes 0 to

00:04:52.760 --> 00:04:54.070
1.

00:04:54.080 --> 00:04:56.070
And basically, we we take the first

00:04:56.080 --> 00:04:58.430
event, we work out how it's going to

00:04:58.440 --> 00:05:00.790
change the state and after what delay,

00:05:00.800 --> 00:05:03.830
we add that to the to the uh

00:05:03.840 --> 00:05:05.750
to the list, and we keep executing

00:05:05.760 --> 00:05:07.750
through that until the simulation has

00:05:07.760 --> 00:05:09.710
finished. So, that's how a discrete

00:05:09.720 --> 00:05:12.110
event simulation works. And as I say,

00:05:12.120 --> 00:05:14.390
there are some limitations. In theory,

00:05:14.400 --> 00:05:16.470
events can occur concurrently. In

00:05:16.480 --> 00:05:18.510
practice, discrete at least most discrete

00:05:18.520 --> 00:05:19.670
event

00:05:19.680 --> 00:05:21.670
simulators will actually do them one

00:05:21.680 --> 00:05:23.510
after the other.

00:05:23.520 --> 00:05:25.950
Um so, uh what about it? Well, the

00:05:25.960 --> 00:05:27.430
management of the simulation time, that

00:05:27.440 --> 00:05:28.990
is the ordering of events. We keep them

00:05:29.000 --> 00:05:30.230
in the order.

00:05:30.240 --> 00:05:31.590
We have a two-step model of time

00:05:31.600 --> 00:05:33.150
propagation. We propagate all the

00:05:33.160 --> 00:05:35.310
signals uh receiving values at this

00:05:35.320 --> 00:05:36.350
time.

00:05:36.360 --> 00:05:37.630
And then we evaluate all of the

00:05:37.640 --> 00:05:39.150
different components in the circuit

00:05:39.160 --> 00:05:41.030
affected by these inputs. So, you

00:05:41.040 --> 00:05:42.750
evaluate all of the components affected

00:05:42.760 --> 00:05:45.230
by the inputs and generate and schedule

00:05:45.240 --> 00:05:47.030
some future events

00:05:47.040 --> 00:05:48.670
uh which basically correspond to when

00:05:48.680 --> 00:05:49.910
their outputs are going to change onto

00:05:49.920 --> 00:05:51.510
the event list. This is a very general

00:05:51.520 --> 00:05:53.910
model not just used for digital circuit

00:05:53.920 --> 00:05:57.070
simulation. It's suitable for gate level

00:05:57.080 --> 00:05:57.830
um

00:05:57.840 --> 00:05:59.470
or RTL level which is what we're going to

00:05:59.480 --> 00:06:02.670
be doing or processor level simulations.

00:06:02.680 --> 00:06:04.790
And you can describe any at any level

00:06:04.800 --> 00:06:06.510
you can describe a component in terms of

00:06:06.520 --> 00:06:08.110
its input events,

00:06:08.120 --> 00:06:10.270
computation of output events from those

00:06:10.280 --> 00:06:14.070
and the propagation delays.

00:06:14.080 --> 00:06:15.590
Uh and there is some are some

00:06:15.600 --> 00:06:17.550
trade-offs. We can we can trade off

00:06:17.560 --> 00:06:20.430
accuracy various various against speed.

00:06:20.440 --> 00:06:22.909
So if you want greater detail, we would

00:06:22.919 --> 00:06:24.790
generate more events. We might actually

00:06:24.800 --> 00:06:27.310
start simulating down at a lower level.

00:06:27.320 --> 00:06:28.630
Right. So bear in mind if you're if

00:06:28.640 --> 00:06:30.870
you're simulating at a high level, you

00:06:30.880 --> 00:06:32.790
might ignore the details of individual

00:06:32.800 --> 00:06:35.190
gates and just treat a component in

00:06:35.200 --> 00:06:37.950
terms of the delay from you know delay

00:06:37.960 --> 00:06:39.070
from the input to output. It might not

00:06:39.080 --> 00:06:40.790
be fully accurate. So there is a there

00:06:40.800 --> 00:06:42.710
is a there is a trade-off here between

00:06:42.720 --> 00:06:46.070
detail how how far down you go and the

00:06:46.080 --> 00:06:47.150
speed at which you can do the

00:06:47.160 --> 00:06:48.950
simulation.

00:06:48.960 --> 00:06:50.430
Um if you've less detail, you've got

00:06:50.440 --> 00:06:52.110
obviously got fewer events to simulate

00:06:52.120 --> 00:06:52.870
it.

00:06:52.880 --> 00:06:53.870
So

00:06:53.880 --> 00:06:56.070
just as I say, the the big thing that

00:06:56.080 --> 00:06:57.110
you have to be aware of that the

00:06:57.120 --> 00:06:59.270
simulation is actually sequential. It

00:06:59.280 --> 00:07:01.430
does one thing at a time.

00:07:01.440 --> 00:07:03.230
The real world circuit you've got is

00:07:03.240 --> 00:07:05.909
concurrent.

00:07:05.919 --> 00:07:07.270
And so

00:07:07.280 --> 00:07:10.070
that they may not always behave the same

00:07:10.080 --> 00:07:12.030
exactly.

00:07:12.040 --> 00:07:14.070
If we are using sequential circuit

00:07:14.080 --> 00:07:15.909
design synchronous circuit design which

00:07:15.919 --> 00:07:17.230
is what we're going to be focusing on in

00:07:17.240 --> 00:07:20.070
this course, that won't become an issue

00:07:20.080 --> 00:07:22.950
um because we're we're using the the

00:07:22.960 --> 00:07:25.070
clock to control the timing of things.

00:07:25.080 --> 00:07:26.550
But if you were designing asynchronous

00:07:26.560 --> 00:07:27.950
circuits,

00:07:27.960 --> 00:07:29.909
the exact delays matter and the exact

00:07:29.919 --> 00:07:31.870
delays in a real in a real circuit may

00:07:31.880 --> 00:07:34.390
be may actually be subject to

00:07:34.400 --> 00:07:36.710
a variation due to temperature and um

00:07:36.720 --> 00:07:38.430
thing which will affect noise and and

00:07:38.440 --> 00:07:40.470
when things transition. So

00:07:40.480 --> 00:07:42.710
uh bear in mind as I said that that

00:07:42.720 --> 00:07:44.350
there's not exactly the same as the real

00:07:44.360 --> 00:07:45.350
world.

00:07:45.360 --> 00:07:46.710
So, that's really it. That's all I

00:07:46.720 --> 00:07:49.950
wanted to cover on how simulations work

00:07:49.960 --> 00:07:53.070
uh, for digital systems.

